          ]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "iterative is the lowest-area family in the registry (shared datapath, no pipeline registers). It is the only family that can plausibly minimise luts_plus_ffs while still meeting the >=1 MSPS throughput constraint at a realistic Fmax. I narrow data_width to 12-16 and n_iter to 10-14 because the error constraint (<= 2^-10) is the binding accuracy requirement; W=12 with N~12 typically gives ~10-11 bits of accuracy, and going much wider or deeper wastes area without helping the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs). angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small (0-2) since extra guard bits add area but only marginally improve accuracy beyond the 2^-10 floor. Both rounding modes are included to let the search find the cheapest rounding that still meets the error bound."
    }
  ,
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 16
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 14
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k trades some area (k parallel datapath elements) for higher throughput per cycle. If iterative at a given W/N cannot hit >=1 MSPS at its Fmax, unrolled_k with k=2-4 can multiply throughput by k at the cost of roughly kx the datapath LUTs. I keep the same W/N/guard ranges as iterative so the accuracy trade-off is comparable, and limit k to 2-4 because k>4 would multiply area too aggressively for a spec where area is the primary objective. This family covers the part of the Pareto front where a slightly larger area buys enough throughput margin to allow a lower-Fmax (and thus potentially lower-area) configuration, or where iterative simply cannot meet 1 MSPS."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated (min luts_plus_ffs) with a hard throughput floor of 1 MSPS and a hard accuracy floor of 2^-10. Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me. iterative is the natural starting point because it has the smallest area footprint; the question is whether its Fmax is high enough to deliver 1 MSPS. unrolled_k is the fallback that buys throughput with area. I focus the search on the narrow W=12-16, N=10-14 window because the 2^-10 error bound is tight enough that W<12 or N<10 will likely violate it, while W>16 or N>14 adds area without improving the selection objective. Guard bits are kept minimal (0-2) to avoid paying area for accuracy headroom the spec does not require. Both rounding modes are included because truncation is cheaper and may still meet the error bound, while rounding may be needed at the edge of the N/W range. The budget is split between the two families so that if iterative cannot meet throughput, unrolled_k results are already available to map the feasible front. Since no fleet facts exist yet, I allocate the full 100-eval budget across these two families in a single round to get an initial Pareto picture before deciding on any refinement."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established and robust. The minimum area design (iterative, 253 LUTs+FFs) comfortably meets the throughput constraint (13.2 MSPS >> 1 MSPS) and the accuracy constraint (10.13 bits > 10 bits). The front extends to higher accuracy (11.9 bits) with modest area increases, providing a good trade-off curve. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum luts_plus_ffs (253) as per the spec's selection rule."
,
  "next_plan": null
}
